s**\n```pseudocode\nCLASS TargetManager\n    VARIABLE targets\n\n    METHOD __init__()\n        targets = []\n\n    METHOD spawn_target()\n        target_position = (RANDOM(0, 800), RANDOM(0, 600))\n        ADD target_position to targets\n\n    METHOD move_targets()\n        FOR EACH target IN targets\n            target_position = (target[0] + RANDOM(-1, 1), target[1] + RANDOM(-1, 1))\n            UPDATE target with new position\n```\n**Key Functionality**: The `TargetManager` class is responsible for spawning new targets at random positions and moving them randomly on the screen.  \n**Third-party Library**: Utilizes the Python `random` module for generating random positions.\n", '|Case|:**player_controls_shooter**\n```pseudocode\nCLASS Shooter\n    VARIABLE position\n\n    METHOD aim(mouse_position: TUPLE)\n        position = mouse_position\n\n    METHOD shoot()\n        PRINT "Shooting at", position\n```\n**Key Functionality**: The `Shooter` class allows the player to aim at a specific position on the screen (derived from mouse input) and execute a shooting action.  \n**Third-party Library**: Uses `pygame` to retrieve mouse position and manage game rendering and input.\n|Case|:**restart_game**\n```pseudocode\nCLASS Game\n    METHOD __init__()\n        // Re-initialize all components of the game\n\n    METHOD restart_game()\n        __init__() // Reset the game state\n```\n**Key Functionality**: The `restart_game` method resets the game to its initial state, preparing it for a new session.  \n**Third-party Library**: Not applicable; functionality is based on standard Python class behavior.']
